{2}(t)\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186522954 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186522977 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186522954 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186522954 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/94/948754761eebc091cffa3aade21d37c92dfa38d69df076bcbb59ee03a06330cf.png)\n\nLet $D$ be the region enclosed by the curve $C$ parametrized as\n$r(t)=⟨2\\sin(2t),\\,6\\cos(t)⟩$ for $t\\in [-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}].$ \nThe area of $D$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\text{Area}(D)={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})dA={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}\\boxed{}\\,dt,\n$$\n\nwhere $P(x,y)=-y$ and $Q(x,y)=0.$ Determine the pointwise integrand obtained from the given $P$, $Q$, and parametrization using Green's theorem, with the sign adjusted for the orientation of $C$.\n\n*Hint: Note that $C$ is negatively oriented.*" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186522954 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "552e2a8e-e82a-5c18-8e63-f2ebfa49d9fa" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186522954 #edb/ref 1074 "Our simple, piecewise smooth, closed path $C$ is negatively oriented, and  \n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=0-(-1)=1.\n$$\n\nSo, according to Green's theorem, we can write the area of the region $D$ enclosed by the curve $C$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(D) & ={∬}_{D}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & =-{∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nNotice that we have to take the line integral with a minus sign since the path is negatively oriented.\n\nSubstituting $P(x,y)=-y$ and $Q(x,y)=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n-{∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y={∮}_{C}y\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe derivative of $r(t)$ is\n\n$$\n{r}^{′}(t)=⟨4\\cos(2t),\\,-6\\sin(t)⟩.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our line integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}y\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}6\\cos(t)\\cdot 4\\cos(2t)\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}24\\cos(t)\\cos(2t)\\,dt.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186522969 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186523013 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186522969 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186522969 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/61/611311e6976285f7e656ae16812999e6abd30184a5823abe0bb9d7cb4290d22e.png)\n\nLet $D$ be the region enclosed by the curve $C$ parametrized as\n$r(t)=⟨2\\sin(t),\\,6\\cos(t)⟩$ for $t\\in [0,2\\pi ].$ \nThe area of $D$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\text{Area}(D)={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})dA={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\boxed{}\\,dt,\n$$\n\nwhere $P(x,y)=-y$ and $Q(x,y)=0.$ Determine the pointwise integrand obtained from the given $P$, $Q$, and parametrization using Green's theorem, with the sign adjusted for the orientation of $C$.\n\n*Hint: Note that $C$ is negatively oriented.*" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186522969 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "7cb47c27-803c-5acc-af12-c1cffbaf193e" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186522969 #edb/ref 1074 "Our simple, smooth, closed path $C$ is negatively oriented, and  \n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=0-(-1)=1.\n$$\n\nSo, according to Green's theorem, we can write the area of the region $D$ enclosed by the curve $C$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(D) & ={∬}_{D}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =-{∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nNotice that we have to take the line integral with a minus sign since the path is negatively oriented.\n\nSubstituting $P(x,y)=-y$ and $Q(x,y)=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n-{∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y={∮}_{C}y\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe derivative of $r(t)$ is\n\n$$\n{r}^{′}(t)=⟨2\\cos(t),\\,-6\\sin(t)⟩.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our line integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}y\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }6\\cos(t)\\cdot 2\\cos(t)\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }12\\cos^{2}(t)\\,dt.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186522980 #edb