ef 17592186045417] [17592186560913 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186560485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186560913 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186560493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186560913 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186560614 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186560913 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186560684 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186560913 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186560729 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186560913 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186560913 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186560913 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186560729 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186560913 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "75e5af1d-ea30-5a37-83f7-6f329a1a3e3b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186560921 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186560422 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186560921 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186560525 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186560921 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186560605 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186560921 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186560625 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186560921 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186560716 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186560921 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186560921 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186560921 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186560525 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186560921 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "8b9b5f42-303d-5796-888d-e11c20c8cac2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186560962 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186560464 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186560962 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186560539 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186560962 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186560583 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186560962 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186560741 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186560962 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186560799 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186560962 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186560962 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186560962 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186560464 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186560962 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "ea3760f2-d10c-5617-a3f3-b8335b0f89d2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186560984 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186560962 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186560984 #edb/ref 1069 "q-126572" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186560984 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186560984 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $C$ be a simple, piecewise-smooth, closed, and positively oriented curve. Given that the line integral\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r\n$$\n\n \nrepresents the area of the region $D$ enclosed by any such curve in ${R}^{2},$ which of the following could be the vector field $F(x,y)?$\n\nI. $F(x,y)=⟨{e}^{y}-y,\\,x{e}^{y}⟩$\n\nII. $F(x,y)=⟨{e}^{y},\\,0⟩$\n\nIII. $F(x,y)=⟨-\\frac{y}{5}+{x}^{7},\\,\\frac{6x}{5}⟩$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186560984 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "2d6a46ea-e810-5ed6-9d15-12e3845c1e76" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186560984 #edb/ref 1074 "According to Green's theorem, if $F(x,y)=⟨P(x,y),\\,Q(x,y)⟩,$ where\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=1,\n$$\n\nwe can write the area of the region $D$ enclosed by the curve $C$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(D) & ={∬}_{D}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each pair in turn.\n\n- Vector field I give the required area. In this case,\n$\\frac{∂P}{∂y}={e}^{y}-1$\nand\n$\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}={e}^{y},$\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=1.\n$$\n\n- Vector field II does *not* give the required area. Notice that\n$\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=-{e}^{y}$\nand\n$\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=0,$\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}={e}^{y}\\ne 1.\n$$\n\n- Vector field III does *not* give the required area. Notice that\n$\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=-\\frac{1}{5}$\nand\n$\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=\\frac{6}{5},$\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{7}{5}\\ne 1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186561018 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186560921 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186561018 #edb/ref 1069 "q-126570" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186561018 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186561018 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $C$ be a simple, piecewise-smooth, closed, and positively oriented curve. Given that the line integral\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y\n$$\n\n \nrepresents the area of the region $D$ enclosed by any such curve in ${R}^{2},$ which of the following could be the functions $P(x,y)$ and $Q(x,y)?$\n\nI. $P(x,y)=-\\frac{y}{3}$ and $Q(x,y)=\\frac{x}{3}$\n\nII. $P(x,y)=y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ and $Q(x,y)=x-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$\n\nIII. $P(x,y)={y}^{2}$ and $Q(x,y)={x}^{2}$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186561018 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "7d76f5fb-ba28-5b32-904d-cbb78731c152" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186561018 #edb/ref 1074 "According to Green's theorem, if \n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=1,\n$$\n\nwe can write the area of the region $D$ enclosed by the curve $C$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(D) & ={∬}_{D}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each pair in turn.\n\n- Pair I does *not* give the required area. In this case, \n$\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=-\\frac{1}{3}$\nand\n$\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=\\frac{1}{3},$\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{2}{3}\\ne 1.\n$$\n\n- Pair II gives the required area. Notice that\n$\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$\nand\n$\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=1.\n$$\n\n- Pair III does *not* give the required area. Notice that \n$\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=2y$\nand\n$\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=2x,$\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=2x-2y\\ne 1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186561027 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186560913 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186561027 #edb/ref 1069 "q-126869" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186561027 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186561027 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/c5/c5eb703c2812d82848bb5a1639c2840c4abf9318b988087e2216d9881137da72.png)\n\nConsider the region $D$ enclosed by the curve $C={C}_{1}\\cup {C}_{2},$ where \n\n- ${C}_{1}$ is parametrized by ${r}_{1}(t)={t}^{4}\\,i+{t}^{3}\\,j$ for $t\\in [-1,1],$ and\n\n- ${C}_{2}$ is parametrized by ${r}_{2}(t)=\\,i-t\\,j$ for $t\\in [-1,1].$\n\nUse Green's theorem to find the area of the region." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186561027 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "8d510a6d-f96b-598f-8f2e-31efe6a6d012" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186561027 #edb/ref 1074 "First, let $P(x,y)=-y$ and $Q(x,y)=0.$ \n\nOur simple, piecewise-smooth, closed path $C$ is negatively oriented, and   \n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=0-(-1)=1.\n$$\n\nSo, according to Green's theorem, we can write the area of the region $D$ enclosed by the curve $C$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(D) & ={∬}_{D}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & =-{∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nNotice that we have to take the line integral with a minus sign since our path is negatively oriented.\n\nSubstituting $P(x,y)=-y$ and $Q(x,y)=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n-{∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y={∮}_{C}y\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe derivatives of ${r}_{1}(t)$ and ${r}_{2}(t)$ are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{C}_{1}:\\, & {r}_{1}^{′}(t)=4{t}^{3}\\,i+3{t}^{2}\\,j \\\\ {C}_{2}:\\, & {r}_{2}^{′}(t)=-j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our line integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}y\\,\\text{d}x & ={∮}_{{C}_{1}\\cup {C}_{2}}y\\,dx \\\\  & ={∮}_{{C}_{1}}y\\,dx+{∮}_{{C}_{2}}y\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{t}^{3}\\cdot 4{t}^{3}\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{-1}^{1}(-t)\\cdot 0\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{t}^{6}\\,\\text{d}t-0 \\\\  & =4[\\frac{{t}^{7}}{7}]_{-1}^{1} \\\\  & =4\\cdot \\frac{2}{7} \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186561062 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186560865 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186561062 #edb/ref 1069 "q-126561" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186561062 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186561062 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $C$ be a simple, piecewise-smooth, closed, and positively oriented curve. Given that the line integral\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y\n$$\n\n \nrepresents the area of the region $D$ enclosed by any such curve in ${R}^{2},$ which of the following could be the functions $P(x,y)$ and $Q(x,y)?$\n\nI. $P(x,y)={e}^{y}$ and $Q(x,y)={e}^{x}$\n\nII. $P(x,y)=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡y$ and $Q(x,y)=y$\n\nIII. $P(x,y)=-y$ and $Q(x,y)=y$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186561062 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "f61d70b2-c968-54d9-b087-2ae80ef01b89" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186561062 #edb/ref 1074 "According to Green's theorem, if \n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=1,\n$$\n\nwe can write the area of the region $D$ enclosed by the curve $C$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(D) & ={∬}_{D}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each pair in turn.\n\n- Pair I does *not* give the required area. In this case, \n$\\frac{∂P}{∂y}={e}^{y}$\nand\n$\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}={e}^{x},$\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}={e}^{x}-{e}^{y}\\ne 1.\n$$\n\n- Pair II does *not* give the required area.  Notice that \n$\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{1}{1+{y}^{2}}$\nand\n$\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=0,$\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=-\\frac{1}{1+{y}^{2}}\\ne 1.\n$$\n\n- Pair III gives the required area. Notice that\n$\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=-1$\nand\n$\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=0,$\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186561063 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186560901 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186561063 #edb/ref 1069 "q-126559" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186561063 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186561063 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $C$ be a simple, piecewise-smooth, closed, and positively oriented curve. Given that the line integral\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y\n$$\n\n \nrepresents the area of the region $D$ enclosed by any such curve in ${R}^{2},$ which of the following could be the functions $P(x,y)$ and $Q(x,y)?$\n\nI. $P(x,y)=y$ and $Q(x,y)=x$\n\nII. $P(x,y)=-y$ and $Q(x,y)=0$\n\nIII. $P(x,y)=y+{e}^{x}$ and $Q(x,y)=\\frac{y}{3}$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186561063 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "f892a9d1-561b-51b8-a283-3b892f8dc787" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186561063 #edb/ref 1074 "According to Green's theorem, if \n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=1,\n$$\n\nwe can write the area of the region $D$ enclosed by the curve $C$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(D) & ={∬}_{D}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each pair in turn.\n\n- Pair I does *not* give the required area. In this case, \n$\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=1$\nand\n$\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=1,$\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=0\\ne 1.\n$$\n\n- Pair II gives the required area. Notice that\n$\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=-1$\nand\n$\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=0,$\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=1.\n$$\n\n- Pair III does *not* give the required area. Notice that\n$\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=1$\nand\n$\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=0,$\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=-1\\ne 1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186561066 #edb/ref 